# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_dev_exempt_unset_vs_empty.py" "DEV-EXEMPT UNSET-VS-EMPTY GATE: PASS" "dev-exempt unset vs explicitly-empty" "dev-exempt unset vs explicitly-empty allowlist (small-findings root fix, SECURITY-adjacent: it governs the quota-wall exemption): the dev-exemption allowlist (core._dev_exempt_account_ids) was read via the generic _policy_text, which collapses BOTH 'no policy row' AND 'row present but blank' onto its DEFAULT — so storing an EMPTY allowlist (the owner's explicit 'exempt NOBODY') silently fell through to the default PO TRIO and RE-EXEMPTED the PO's own accounts instead of nobody (the WRONG direction for a wall knob — clearing an exemption must REMOVE it). ROOT fix: the reader now reads core.policy DIRECTLY (owner-pinned, FORCE-RLS) and branches on ROW EXISTENCE (the plpgsql FOUND flag), distinguishing the three cases: UNSET (no row) -> the default trio; EXPLICITLY-EMPTY (row='') -> exempt NOBODY; a SET list -> exactly that list. Proven end-to-end at the quota wall (after clearing, a previously-exempt PO account is now WALLED) and FAIL-CLOSED preserved (a broken read still walls)" "dev-exempt unset-vs-explicitly-empty (clearing the dogfood allowlist now exempts NOBODY, not the PO trio; unset still defaults to the trio; a set list is exact; FAIL-CLOSED intact) — quota-wall correctness" 18
